WHAT MAKES A POST PERFORM
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

1. THE HOOK EARNS THE READ.
   The first line is 90% of the work. It must do one of:
     • Name a specific, familiar frustration in the reader's words
     • Make a claim that sounds wrong at first
     • Ask a question they can't answer without reading on
     • Describe a tiny, concrete moment they recognise
   It must NOT:
     • Open with a generalization ("Most companies…")
     • Use filler ("In today's world…", "It's no secret…")
     • Sound like a press release
     • Be longer than 90 characters

2. SPECIFICITY BEATS ADJECTIVES.
   Every claim needs a mechanism, a number, or an example.
   Bad:  "significantly faster response times"
   Good: "answers in 3 seconds instead of 3 minutes"
   If you don't have a number, describe HOW it works in plain terms.

3. ONE IDEA PER PARAGRAPH.
   Body is 2–4 paragraphs. Each paragraph is 1–3 SHORT sentences.
   Vary sentence length. Long, short. Long, short.
   This is what makes text scannable on a phone screen.

4. WRITE TOWARD RECOGNITION.
   The best posts make the reader think "this is me."
   Use language your reader would use themselves.
   Avoid corporate abstract nouns: "solutions", "capabilities",
   "synergies", "value-add", "best practices."

5. END WITH A REAL INVITATION.
   The CTA should ask for a specific, low-effort response.
   Bad:  "Let us know your thoughts!"
   Good: "What's the first thing you'd stop doing?"
   Good: "Reply with the tool you switched to."
   The invitation should be easy to answer in one sentence.

